@@ -0,0 +1,87 @@
+# Daily Grind security audit and deployment
+
+The audit covered all three original files: `index.html`, `server.js`, and
+`package.json`. No browser or listening server was used.
+
+## Findings and changes
+
+- There are no browser scripts, event handlers, DOM injection sinks, messaging,
+  analytics, frames, OAuth/payment popups, cookies, or browser storage. All links
+  are local. No sanitizer or client-side JavaScript dependency is needed.
+- Inline CSS and one `style` attribute were moved to `/styles.css` without changing
+  the design. The only remote dependency is the existing HTTPS Unsplash background
+  image. CSP allows its exact origin, local CSS and images, and same-origin report
+  connections; all other resource types default to denied.
+- Previously the server only sent Content-Type. Every response now receives CSP,
+  Trusted Types restrictions, clickjacking protection, nosniff, a referrer policy,
+  unused-feature restrictions, COOP and CORP. Scripts and inline styles are denied;
+  Trusted Types policy creation is denied. No nonce/hash is needed because there
+  are no scripts or inline styles left to authorize. Browsers that do not support
+  Trusted Types still get CSP; the site requires no Trusted Types JavaScript API.
+- Fetch Metadata rejects cross-origin resource requests while allowing top-level
+  GET/HEAD navigation. Same-site subdomains are not implicitly trusted. Missing
+  Fetch Metadata is permitted for older clients; it is not an authentication check.
+  `Vary` covers the relevant metadata, and responses use `no-store` during rollout.
+- `app.js` contains the request handler without opening a socket. Asset filenames
+  are explicitly selected; URL and Host input cannot select filesystem paths or
+  reporting destinations. MIME types are explicit, including errors and CSS.
+- CSP reporting accepts modern and legacy report formats, limits bodies to 16 KiB
+  and batches to 20 entries, and logs only validated directive/disposition fields.
+  It never logs full reports, URLs, query strings, cookies, user agents or samples.
+  There is deliberately no `report-sample` token because script samples could
+  contain private data. Do not enable raw request-body logging at the proxy.
+
+## Rollout
+
+The default `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=enforce` applies the complete policy to this
+audited, script-free site. To stage a future change with unknown dependencies,
+configure `SECURITY_POLICY_MODE=report-only` in the deployment environment.
+That mode keeps object/base restrictions and clickjacking protection enforced,
+but puts the full CSP, including Trusted Types, in the report-only header.
+Other companion headers remain enforced. Unknown mode values fail startup.
+
+Set `PUBLIC_ORIGIN` to the site's canonical HTTPS origin, for example
+`https://coffee.example`. This enables an absolute `Reporting-Endpoints` target
+and a cautious `Strict-Transport-Security: max-age=300`. This value is never
+derived from Host or forwarded headers. Without it, legacy CSP `report-uri`
+still targets `/security-reports`, but modern Reporting API delivery and HSTS
+are not configured. Reporting API delivery depends on browser support and a
+secure context; reports are best-effort, unauthenticated signals.
+
+Terminate TLS at the production proxy, redirect HTTP to HTTPS there, forward
+`/security-reports` POST requests to this handler, and preserve security headers
+on documents, styles, and error responses. Add a proxy-level rate/body limit
+for report ingestion, bounded log retention, and restrict direct access to the
+upstream. This repository does not contain an edge/CDN configuration or a TLS
+certificate; application headers alone do not configure HTTPS. Gradually increase
+HSTS duration after confirming HTTPS coverage. Do not add `includeSubDomains`
+or `preload` until every affected subdomain is ready.
+
+Collect report-only traffic over representative days/weeks before promoting a
+changed policy to enforcement. Compare reported directive failures with deployed
+markup, account for extension/old-client noise, then switch the environment mode
+to `enforce`. Retain reporting after enforcement. Revert to `report-only` to
+investigate resource-policy regressions; do not add `unsafe-inline`, `unsafe-eval`,
+broad script origins, or pass-through Trusted Types policies as a shortcut.
+
+COOP uses `same-origin-allow-popups`; CORP is `same-origin`. COEP/document isolation
+is intentionally omitted: there is no shared-memory feature requiring it, and it
+could break external images. Re-audit popup, embedding and cross-site integrations
+before adding any. There are no third-party scripts/styles requiring SRI and no
+cross-origin APIs requiring CORS permissions.
+
+`/logout` remains the existing text-only placeholder. There is no session to
+invalidate. Before implementing authentication, use secure HttpOnly SameSite
+cookies, server-side session invalidation, CSRF-protected POST logout, and scoped
+Clear-Site-Data as appropriate. Do not claim GET logout or client-side clearing
+alone terminates a session. Other navigation paths retain the original landing
+page fallback.
+
+## Automated checks
+
+`npm run build` performs syntax checks only. `npm test` exercises the listener-free
+handler with in-memory streams, including markup/CSP compatibility, both policy
+modes, response headers, Fetch Metadata, reporting privacy and limits, trusted
+origin validation, and routing. Neither command starts a server or imports
+`server.js`. These checks do not prove production proxy/TLS configuration or
+browser policy enforcement; production telemetry remains part of rollout.
